    "case_id": "3",
    "case_summary": "A female patient in her early 50s presented with multiple (approximately 10) bluish, partially keratotic papules and nodules on the lower legs. The lesions had been growing very slowly in number and size since age 24 and had been bleeding occasionally after trauma. The patient used a wheelchair due to right-sided hemiparesis and hemianopsia caused by an intracranial hemorrhage. At age 4, she had recurrent seizures. She had lipolymphedema of the lower legs attributed to functional venous insufficiency with insufficient venous pump activity, and had been wearing compression stockings for years. Family history revealed similar conditions in the patient's father and aunt, with the father having died of an intracranial hemorrhage in his early 70s.\n\nMultiple bluish, partially keratotic papules and nodules on the lower legs. Lipolymphedema of the lower legs was present. Right-sided hemiparesis and hemianopsia were noted.\n\n- Sonography: Demonstrated intradermal slow-flow vascular malformations with a maximum diameter of 1.2 cm.\n\n- Histopathology:\n  Image Description: Biopsy revealed a well-circumscribed lesion with hyperkeratosis and acanthosis of the epidermis overlying a conglomerate of dilated venous-like malformations with continuous smooth muscle in the upper dermis, partly showing thrombosis.\n\n- Genetic Testing:\n  Result: Detection of a heterozygous pathogenic KRIT1 variant.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Cerebral Cavernous Malformations (CCM)\nB. Venous Malformation Syndrome\nC. Familial cerebral cavernous malformations\nD. Cerebral Cavernous Malformation (CCM1)\n",
